  "camp_d_strengths": [
    "Successfully dropped the unviable ANOHNI book claim, preventing further factual loss of credibility.",
    "Solidified the 6-member bloc around the editorial intent argument, specifically that 'Sister Ray' (intravenous narcotics mainline) cannot be central to a lighthearted secondary-school comedy.",
    "The 1:1 tripartite mapping of the songs to Euphoria's female leads (Rue/Kat/Jules) remains our most aesthetically and narratively compelling structural argument."
  ],
  "camp_o_counter_arguments": [
    "Camp O countered the 'Walk on the Wild Side Paradox' by arguing that WOTWS is a solo Reed track, which would have collapsed the two-step clue structure (Band -> Solo career) and spoiled 'Transformer'.",
    "They cited Lou Reed's own quotes characterizing 'Sister Ray' as an anthem of drag queens, oral sex ('duck and suck'), and shedding bourgeois moral shame, attempting to neutralize the drug angle.",
    "They doubled down on the binary verification rule: trivia stems never invent non-existent books, whereas Ezra Furman's 33 1/3 volume on Transformer and scoring of Sex Education is an unassailable factual record."
  ]
},

"strategic_breakthrough_for_round_7": {
  "dismantling_camp_o_rebuttal": [
    "Camp O's excuse for avoiding 'Walk on the Wild Side' was that it is a solo track and they needed Velvet Underground songs. BUT if the author needed a Velvet Underground track about adolescent transgender identity and dysphoria for Sex Education, they had 'Candy Says' (1969, The Velvet Underground)—literally Lou Reed's most poignant, gentle song about Candy Darling hating her body ('Candy says I've come to hate my body / and all that it requires in this world').",
    "Why bypass 'Candy Says'—a VU track that perfectly captures the gentle, tender, empathetic adolescent dysphoria of Sex Education—to specifically mandate 'Sister Ray' (17 minutes of abrasive noise about shooting mainline speed and getting shot) and 'Lady Godiva's Operation' (a violent, grotesque, botched surgical lobotomy)?",
    "Because 'Lady Godiva's Operation' is NOT an affirming transition—it is medical trauma and tragedy. And 'Sister Ray' is harrowing chaos. That dark, abrasive, transgressive tone matches the raw trauma of Euphoria (Rue's overdose, Jules's traumatic hospitalizations and dangerous encounters), NOT Otis Milburn's high school clinic."
  ]
},

"action_plan_for_round_7": {
  "tone": "Scholarly, highly analytical, engaging deeply with musicological text without defensiveness.",
  "execution": [
    "Acknowledge Camp O's clever argument regarding why WOTWS wasn't used (solo vs band distinction).",
    "Deploy the 'Candy Says' counter-stroke: demonstrate that if band-era trans/queer empathy were the clue for Sex Education, 'Candy Says' was the obvious, world-famous choice.",
    "Highlight the visceral horror of 'Lady Godiva's Operation' (a botched, cynical lobotomy/scalpel tragedy) and show how absurd it is to map that onto Sex Education's supportive, affirming health clinic, whereas it mirrors the intense psychological trauma and bodily peril of Jules in Euphoria.",
    "Reinforce the distinction in prompt phrasing ('a major contributor to the music') to keep D viable as an editorial priority of theme over superficial keyword traps.",
    "Keep the 6-member bloc completely locked and attempt to sow doubt in at least 1-2 swing-prone colleagues (like Colleague 6)."
  ]
}
}

